Blood is a reusable fld::blood profile layered on fld::continuum.
It does not create a second fluid solver, collision system, particle system,
or pressure grid.

Files:
  blood.hpp, blood.cpp                  blood material and reusable adapter
  ../continuum/shaders/grid.comp        optional Carreau shear thinning and floor drag
  ../continuum/shaders/surface.comp     FLD_BLOOD optical shading (same renderer)

Usage:
  fld::blood::material profile{};
  profile.viscosity_zero=.056f;
  profile.viscosity_high=.00345f;
  profile.relaxation_seconds=3.313f;
  profile.shear_exponent=.3568f;
  profile.substrate_drag=4.f;
  profile.shallow_color={.71f,.046f,.038f};
  profile.optical_density=32.f;
  auto programs=fld::blood::shaders("build/shaders/");
  fld::blood::init(options,drop,programs,frames,width,height,profile);
  fld::blood::record(command,frame,dt,render_input);
  fld::blood::shutdown();

Material units: density kg/m^3, viscosity Pa*s, surface tension N/m,
relaxation s, contact drag 1/s. The example uses approximately 1060 kg/m^3,
Carreau mu_0=.056 Pa*s, mu_infinity=.00345 Pa*s, lambda=3.313 s,
n=.3568 (published whole-blood reference set, not a universal constant).
These are illustrative blood rheology values; hematocrit and temperature
change actual blood properties. The extra contact drag is a game-look
approximation, not an intrinsic medical parameter.

The optical layer combines transmitted background with red-selective
absorption. Thin layers are brighter and more translucent; thicker layers
become darker, more opaque burgundy. Colors and optical density are editable
in the blood material. Water shaders remain available as the normal
continuum rendering variant.

Not simulated: clots, drying, cellular blood flow, fine splashes, true
contact-angle hysteresis or medically validated rheology. This is a
single-fluid real-time approximation with numerical limits of the existing
VOF/MAC pressure solver. GPU runtime and 60 FPS are not yet validated.

2026-11-05 implementation notes
The first test used a 0.68m sphere (about 1,317 litres) on a 14.5cm grid.
It inevitably resembled a red carpet rather than a localized blood pool.
The game now uses a 0.22m sphere (~45 litres) and a local 4cm grid.
This is still a large *test pool*, not a physiological blood droplet.

Contact/wetting corrections:
  Voxelized collider cells are blocked at >50% volume overlap instead of
  at any overlap, reducing the padded moat around boxes.
  An approximate advancing/receding contact-angle selection uses local
  tangential flow. A true sub-cell dynamic wetting law is not yet implemented.
  Surface film uses a coherent 3x3 VOF neighborhood, excluding diffuse traces.
  Render-only extrapolation avoids drawing gaps at solid boundaries.
  Near-horizontal rays sample the occupied floor-height slab; all rays use
  the same liquid field, not a second independently animated film mesh.
  The upscaler rejects unrelated opaque surfaces by world-space positions
  and rejects fluid samples of inconsistent depth. The blood-only composite
  transfers the optical correction to the sharp full-resolution background.

Literature:
  Xiang et al., Science & Justice (2023):
    https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37453784/
    Early spreading approximates a low-viscosity Newtonian drop;
    later retraction and oscillations depend strongly on wetting and rheology.
  Computational blood Carreau profile:
    https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7906423/
    mu_0=.056, mu_infinity=.00345 Pa*s, lambda=3.313 s, n=.3568.
  Dynamic wetting VOF method (2026):
    https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcp.2026.114969
    Contact-angle hysteresis and contact-line velocity require more
    advanced numerical treatment than this approximate GPU solver.

Important limits:
  A 4cm cell cannot resolve millimetre-scale blood droplet physics,
  thin capillary rims, contact line pinning or drying correctly.
  The first-order VOF advection still diffuses liquid, and 36 fixed Jacobi
  iterations provide no measured incompressibility residual.
  Optical pigments and substrate drag are game approximations.
  No numerical volume-conservation measurement, GPU runtime compile,
  or 60 FPS benchmark has been performed in this environment.
